  "voiceover": "The gospel does not begin in Matthew. Before Israel existed, God made a promise to one wandering man: in you all the families of the earth shall be blessed. Not one nation only — all of them. That promise is older than the law, older than the kingdom, older than the temple. Then, at the centre of Israel's worship, a way of dealing with guilt. On the Day of Atonement, two goats. One is slain. The other is sent away into the wilderness, carrying the people's sins with it. Guilt is not excused. It is transferred, and carried away. But notice what the system never does: it never finishes. Every year, blood again. Every year, two goats again. The letter to the Hebrews reads the repetition as the point — a shadow, it says, that keeps telling you it is a shadow. Then a promise to David: a throne that will not end. Not a passing dynasty. A permanent throne. And in the prophets, a servant who suffers — not for his own wrong, but for the wrongs of others — and who is vindicated afterward. Now put those together. A blessing aimed at every nation. Guilt transferred, and carried away. An atonement made once a year, forever. A king whose reign does not end. A servant who suffers for others. That is the shape of the gospel — not a summary of the New Testament. Every line of it was written centuries before Jesus was born. And the New Testament itself keeps reaching for these texts — Paul says Scripture announced the gospel in advance to Abraham. And this is not a waiting room. It is Israel's real history and Israel's real worship — not a coded message waiting to be cracked. It happened, and it rhymes. Be honest about how Christians read this: backward, in the light of the resurrection — not decoded in advance. It is not a probability proof, and the case for Christ begins at the resurrection. But once you have that, the shape is unmistakable. The gospel was not an afterthought. It was what God had been promising — and picturing — out loud for a thousand years.",
